diff --git a/lib/scheduler/kaiair/pktr_grk.h b/lib/scheduler/kaiair/pktr_grk.h
index d23c36e25c..9d45aad0aa 100644
--- a/lib/scheduler/kaiair/pktr_grk.h
+++ b/lib/scheduler/kaiair/pktr_grk.h
@@ -1,37 +1,18 @@
 // SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (C) 2021-2026 Software Radio Systems Limited
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause-Open-MPI
 //
-// KaiAir / PktR, GRK (Gain-Ratio-K) interference model and K-adaptation, the slow (network) loop.
+// KaiAir/PktR GRK (Gain-Ratio-K) interference model and K-adaptation, the slow network loop.
 //
-// The Physical-Ratio-K (PRK) model turns "which nodes interfere with link (T->R)?", impractical to
-// answer by measuring every interferer's contribution, into a single, adaptable gain threshold.
-// A node C is a *significant* interferer for the link (T->R) iff its gain to the receiver is within
-// a factor K of the intended-signal gain:
+// PRK replaces per-interferer measurement with a single adaptable gain threshold. A node C is a
+// significant interferer of link (T,R) when G(C,R) >= G(T,R) / K. Nodes inside this exclusion
+// region are kept from transmitting concurrently by the ONAMA gate, so residual interference
+// comes only from the weaker nodes outside it. A larger K means a larger region, less
+// interference and less spatial reuse.
 //
-//   C in ExclusionRegion(T->R)   <=>   G(C,R) >= G(T,R) / K.
-//
-// Nodes inside the exclusion region (ER) are prevented from transmitting concurrently (by the ONAMA
-// TDMA gate, Phase 5); the residual interference then comes only from the weaker, out-of-ER nodes.
-// K trades reliability against spatial reuse: larger K => larger ER => fewer concurrent transmitters
-// => less interference but lower reuse.
-//
-// K-adaptation sizes the ER so that the expected *residual* interference (from out-of-ER nodes) stays
-// within the budget the power-control loop can tolerate, i.e. E_max[I_R] = S - gamma - sigma*k(beta)
-// from pktr_ipc.h (the dual of the Cantelli transmit-power target). The interference-control error
-//
-//   Delta_I_R = E_max[I_R] - I_current
-//
-// drives it: Delta_I_R < 0 (interference exceeds budget) grows the ER (raises K); Delta_I_R > 0
-// (headroom) lets it shrink (lowers K), reclaiming reuse.
-//
-// This header consumes a *gain map* only as an abstraction: the caller supplies, per candidate
-// interferer C, the gain G(C,R), C's expected transmit power E[P_C], and its activity factor
-// E[alpha_C] (from scheduling statistics). Where those come from, PUSCH/SRS RSRP + PHR for in-cell
-// gains, neighbour-cell SSB-RSRP via TDD reciprocity for cross-cell gains, is the signal-map's job
-// (Phase 1, T1.3/T1.4), not this module's.
-//
-// Units: gains, powers, E_max and I_R are in dB / dBm; interference powers are summed in the linear
-// domain (they add as powers, not in dB). The module is otherwise pure math: no scheduler state.
+// K-adaptation sizes the region so the residual interference stays inside the budget the power
+// loop can tolerate, E_max[I_R] = S - gamma - sigma*k(beta) from pktr_ipc.h. The error
+// E_max[I_R] - I_current drives K up when interference exceeds the budget and lets K shrink,
+// reclaiming reuse, when there is headroom.
 
 #pragma once
 
